# {{ run }}

Bradley-Terry reward model over `{{ cfg.model.base_model }}`, trained on
{% if rm_eval %}{{ rm_eval.n_train_pairs }}{% else %}N{% endif %} preference pairs
({{ (cfg.data.rm_pair_fraction * 100) | round | int }}% of the available data).

Trained as one point on a reward-model-quality axis: the same GRPO recipe is run
against RMs of deliberately different accuracy so that downstream win-rate can be
plotted against reward-model quality rather than assumed to be independent of it.

## Evaluation

{% if rm_eval %}
| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Held-out pairwise accuracy | **{{ "%.4f"|format(rm_eval.eval_accuracy) }}** |
| Training pairs | {{ rm_eval.n_train_pairs }} |
| Data fraction | {{ rm_eval.rm_pair_fraction }} |
{% else %}
_No `rm_eval.json` in this run directory._
{% endif %}

Pairwise accuracy on a held-out split is a weak proxy for reward-model quality:
it says nothing about calibration or about behaviour off the preference-data
distribution, which is exactly where a policy will push it.

## Usage

```python
from transformers import AutoModelForSequenceClassification, AutoTokenizer

tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("{{ run }}")
rm = AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained("{{ run }}", num_labels=1)
score = rm(**tok(f"{prompt}\n\n{response}", return_tensors="pt")).logits[0][0]
```

## Limitations

- Optimising against this model will find its failure modes; treat scores as a
  training signal, not an evaluation metric.
- Trained on a single preference dataset with known verbosity bias.
